  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\n   We will formulate the problem as a constrained BP-hard optimization problem and use a mixed-integer programming (MIP) approach to find an improved lower bound. The key idea is to discretize the step function definition and assign binary variables to define the step transitions. This approach ensures a valid wavelet representation respecting transitions and maintains the required integral and interval constraints.\n\n   The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap with its delayed version. We will use a binary representation to define the steps, which allows us to directly enforce transitions and maintain a valid step function structure. This binary formulation converts the problem into a MIP problem that approximate the continuous version.\n\n   The current record is 0.3808. Our MIP approach will use the initial 45-point solution as a fallback if optimization fails. We will try candidate resolutions matching parent sample point counts for efficient comparison. The search will be driven by the collected overlap coefficient.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\n   - **Representation**: Define a step function with n=90 points by interpolating from the parent solution. Use a piecewise constant function with transition points determined by binary variables.\n   - **Variables**: Let b_i be a binary variable indicating if there is a transition or change in the function value at sample point i. Let h[i] represent the function value at the ith sample.\n   - **Objective**: Minimize:\n        C_5 = dx * max_k |np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')|\n   - **Constraints**:\n     1. $\\sum_{i=1}^{N} h[i] = l$ (integral constraint)\n     2. $\\sum_{i=1}^{N} (h[i] - \\lfloor h[i] \\rfloor) = 0$ (enforce h[i] in [0,1])\n     3. $h[i] \\geq 1$ if b[i]=1 and b[i-1]=0 (transition condition)\n        $h[i] \\leq \\epsilon$ if b[i]=1 and b[i-1]=0 (transition condition)\n        $h[i] = h[i-1]$ if b[i-1]!=1 and b[i]!=1\n     4. Transition smoothness constraints\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\n   - Compute dx for the current resolution (n=90)\n   - Create interpolator function to maintain parent solution structure\n   - Use MIP formulation with binary variables to enforce step changes\n   - Solve using a mixed-integer program\n   - Compare solution's C5 score against current best\n   - Maintain fallback to previous score upon optimization failures\n\n4. Search Choices:\n\n   - Resolution triplet: 45, 90, 180 points to ensure continuity of improvement\n   - Objective evaluated directly for each solution candidate\n   - Use parent-based interpolation for higher resolutions\n\n5. Numerical Robustness:\n\n   - Explicit interpolation for parent to child resolution continuity\n   - Fallback mechanisms to ensure solution availability\n   - Direct evaluation based on parent-provided overlap integral calculation\n\n6. Runtime Safeguards:\n\n   - Early termination if time budget exceeded\n   - Maintain at least one fallback solution at all times\n   - Direct computation of overlap coefficient after each optimization attempt",
